{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/diagonal-line-extrapolation.md", "content": "# Sparse motif: extrapolating a marked diagonal

A solved test contained a uniform background (8), two same-colored cells (2) at `(6,3)` and `(7,4)`, and nearby differently colored cells at `(7,5)` and `(8,5)`. The output extended only the color-2 diagonal backward with step `(-1,-1)` until the left grid boundary, adding 2s at `(5,2)`, `(4,1)`, and `(3,0)`. The original cells, including the other colors, remained unchanged.

Reusable inference: identify repeated same-color offsets in a small seed; test whether that vector defines a ray/line extended through background. Track direction and clipping against grid edges. Do not propagate unrelated adjacent colors or fill the entire diagonal without evidence. In this conversation, the demos were heterogeneous and mostly identity examples, so they did not establish the test's exact rule; the correct prediction came from recognizing the two-cell same-color diagonal and preserving all unrelated cells. This is a case note, not a universal transformation claim.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u030/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/diagonal-line-extrapolation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/sparse-grid-demo-lesson.md", "content": "# Sparse-grid line completion lesson

In a sparse grid with scattered same-color marks, a demo may reveal that only specific aligned interior cells are painted a new color. The useful analysis is cellwise: record changed coordinates, separate changed background cells from original marks, then test row and column runs and their endpoints. Do not infer that every possible aligned pair is filled; verify every qualifying and non-qualifying run in the example. Transfer the smallest rule that exactly reproduces the full demo, preserving all original cells unless the output demonstrates replacement. In the reviewed instance, the test transformation was to paint background cells lying strictly between vertically aligned 0 markers using color 3, with marks and other cells preserved. First demo request gave no validation of this rule; the second demonstrated inserted 3s in line interiors. Exact output feedback confirmed the derived test answer.

Treat this as an evidence-backed pattern, not a universal ARC rule: obtain/compare examples to establish axis, endpoint, and color conditions before applying.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u030/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/sparse-grid-demo-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-evidence-and-retry", "file": "references/identity-demo-interpretation.md", "content": "# Interpreting identity demonstrations

An input/output-identical demonstration is evidence, not a blank example. Record the complete identity result and ask what condition might distinguish it from transformed examples: object configuration, palette roles, dimensions, count, or spatial relation. Do not infer a universal motif-copying or expansion rule from one transformed example if another demo preserves its salient pattern. First test whether a coherent structural trigger explains both. If competing triggers predict different outputs for the test, request a further pair only when it can distinguish them; otherwise avoid a chain of requests that does not improve the test prediction. Replay every full pair under the proposed conditional rule before submitting.

In sparse-marker tasks, distinguish the marker field from a compact multicolor seed/object. A visible seed does not establish that every scattered marker instantiates that motif; test exact change masks and conditions before expansion.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u030/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-evidence-and-retry/references/identity-demo-interpretation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/cross-demo-changed-mask-audit.md", "content": "# Cross-demo changed-mask audit

When demos appear visually unrelated (different dimensions, background colors, seed motifs, and output colors), first compare their exact input/output differences rather than assuming a fixed motif-copy rule. For every pair, record: (1) role-level colors (background, seed, painted color), (2) exact changed-cell coordinates and their bounding boxes, (3) cells left unchanged inside apparent motifs, (4) any connected components or repeated offsets involved, and (5) a concrete rule that predicts both changed and unchanged cells.

A plausible hypothesis is not validated by resemblance. Replay it across *all* demos and require exact agreement, including untouched noise and cells that retain the background. In this session, several demos had completely different grid sizes and seed shapes, and a guessed connected-zero flood-fill failed; that illustrates why a visual/connectedness guess must be checked against demo outputs before submission. After a failed answer, re-evaluate the rule itself and seek a demo that distinguishes explicit alternatives, rather than making another unsupported guess. Keep each demo request tied to a named uncertainty, since requests and wrong submissions both incur cost.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u030/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/cross-demo-changed-mask-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-rejection-recovery", "file": "references/repeated-fallback-loop.md", "content": "# Repeated fallback submissions after rejection

An incorrect submission rejects the complete grid and gives no localization. Repeating the same blank/background-only candidate, or making cosmetic edits to it, cannot add information and wastes attempt budget. Before retrying, identify a materially changed hypothesis and check it against every complete demo, including output shape, role colors, changed-cell mask, and preservation. If no revised rule is supported, request a demonstration only when a concrete observation could distinguish live alternatives and change the test prediction. With heterogeneous demos, compare functional roles and changed masks; do not let one visually salient example override the rest.
