{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/heterogeneous-line-fill-overreach.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous line-fill demos: do not transfer a surface rule

## Signal from this task family
A stream can return demos with unrelated grid sizes, background/foreground colors, and sparse layouts, while one test grid has many aligned marks. It is tempting to infer a generic rule such as “paint every gap between collinear marks.” Even when some demos visibly add a second color in row/column gaps, that rule must not be transferred unless it reproduces the *entire changed-cell mask and unchanged cells in every demo* under the same role-level interpretation.

## Evidence ledger
For each pair, record:
- background and mark/output colors as roles, not fixed values;
- exact coordinates of all input marks;
- every changed output coordinate, grouped by row/column and local motif;
- mark cells and background cells that remain untouched;
- whether a candidate line/segment rule predicts all changed and unchanged locations.

The examples can exhibit added color-2 cells along some aligned gaps, but the test's dense cross-axis alignments make blanket row/column interpolation a high-impact assumption. Repeatedly resubmitting that same gap-fill hypothesis with minor variants is not new evidence and wastes attempts. Do not claim a model is validated merely because it resembles one demonstration.

## Recovery after rejection
After a failed submission, preserve the rejection as evidence against the exact candidate. Identify which competing rule would change the test output, then check whether the existing demonstrations discriminate them. If the demonstrations do not resolve it and demo budget remains, request a discriminating pair; once exhausted, avoid cosmetic replays and submit only a materially different hypothesis supported by the complete set. Prefer a conservative, mechanically checked candidate over copying a previous failed grid.

## Validation gate
Before submit, replay the candidate over every demo pair. It must match output dimensions, all additions/removals/recolors, and all identity/background positions. For line completion specifically, distinguish among: filling only one-cell holes, filling gaps between consecutive markers, filling the entire extrema span, extending beyond endpoints, and triggering only where crossing lines/motifs exist. Never conflate these variants.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u030/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/heterogeneous-line-fill-overreach.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/post-rejection-action-gate.md", "content": "# Gate the next action after rejection

A rejected submission provides only a binary signal; it does not identify which cells were wrong. Do not respond with a speculative near-variant based on the same demos. Revisit the complete model: output shape, transformation trigger, role/color mapping, changed-cell mask, and unchanged regions. If multiple materially different hypotheses remain and the demo budget permits, request a demo chosen to separate them. Submit again only when there is a specific evidence-based model revision that reproduces all demonstrations.

In this instance, two visibly different demo pairs showed localized changes: one recolored only selected 1-cells to 2 while other 8-cells remained, and another filled much of the region bounded by 8 while preserving boundary cells. A test guess that globally filled the region around the 3-cells was rejected. The demos did not validate that broad fill operation on the test; after rejection, the sound next move is targeted evidence gathering rather than arbitrary color or footprint changes.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u030/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/post-rejection-action-gate.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/session-18868900-demo-budget.md", "content": "# Heterogeneous demos: transfer and stopping audit

This session supplied four sharply different examples: a 28×14 input yielding a singleton color, two large periodic/lattice inputs yielding compact 4×6 and 5×3 grids, and a 22×27 lattice yielding 5×3. Their output shapes and palette encodings differ, while the 27×27 test is a periodic 3/4/9/2/7/1 lattice. The singleton output was not a valid basis for the test shape, and a guessed 6×4 block expansion was rejected. These examples demonstrate that output shape must be inferred independently from palette/marker structure and must replay across relevant demos before submission.

Reusable audit: (1) record input and output dimensions and marker coordinates for every pair; (2) distinguish output-shape rule from color/phase rule; (3) normalize geometry and role colors, then require a single candidate to reproduce complete demos; (4) after rejection, reconsider shape and mapping together rather than making a cosmetic variant; (5) request another demo only if an explicit ambiguity remains and the next pair could distinguish candidate outputs. This session did not establish a validated transformation, so do not treat any proposed test output as a known answer.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u030/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/session-18868900-demo-budget.md"}
